From ca4c50757953cc1ca9531f40697e86b70c0bef66 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: mayor <git@hough.ink>
Date: Sat, 18 Jul 2026 16:22:26 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 49/57] fix(polecat): capacity snapshot treats missing-worktree
 polecats as reusable (hq-fqap follow-up)
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The first hq-fqap commit fixed the two workstate gatherers that stat the
worktree (manager.workstateInputForPolecat and the check-recovery
builder), but the capacity snapshot uses a THIRD builder —
buildPolecatInventoryItem — which reads only agent-bead fields with no
git/worktree access. For a nuked polecat whose cleanup_status is
unrecorded (<missing>), that builder produced a cleanup-unknown blocker
-> NEEDS_RECOVERY, so `gt scheduler status` still reported the nuked
polecats as recovery-blocked and kept dispatch starved (recovery=10 of
11) even after the check-recovery verdict flipped to SAFE_TO_NUKE.

Stat the worktree directly in this bulk path too: add polecatWorktreeMissing
(checks both the new polecats/<name>/<rig> and legacy polecats/<name>
layouts for a .git) and thread the result through buildPolecatInventoryItem
into WorkstateInput.WorktreeMissing. A missing worktree means the polecat
was cleanly nuked (no work at risk) -> reusable; a worktree that still
exists with an unrecorded cleanup_status stays recovery-blocked (fail
closed). Bead-level blockers (hook, open MR, active work) are still
honored, so a nuked polecat with an open PR reports PENDING_MR.

Verified live: feryn capacity went from recovery=10 (0 free) to recovery=3
(7 free, 1 reusable idle) with the remaining recovery being genuinely
blocked polecats.

Tests: add nuked-missing-worktree reusable, nuked-open-MR pending, and
present-worktree-unknown-still-recovery cases to the inventory test;
thread the new parameter through capacity/list callers and their tests.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
